    description: "**Measured** by the interface-dispatch parity column (`test_interface_dispatch_parity.py`), on:\n\n    abstract class Shape { int area(); }\n    class Square implements Shape { int area() => 1; }\n\nThe analyzer emits **all four symbols correctly**, with dot-qualified member names that every shared linker can parse:\n\n    class   'Shape'         method  'Shape.area'\n    class   'Square'        method  'Square.area'\n\nand **no inheritance edge whatsoever** — no `implements`, no `extends`, no `inherits`. So `linkers/type_hierarchy` has nothing to build its inheritance maps from and Dart gets no `dispatches_to` edge.\n\nThis is the cleanest of the parity column's eight holes: nothing about naming, nothing about vocabulary, nothing about kinds. One missing producer emission. Contrast php (three stacked defects) and solidity (correct emission, consumer blindness).\n\n**Consequence beyond dispatch.** `implements`/`extends` edges feed inheritance-based reachability, so a Dart class that is only ever used through its interface reads as dead code. Not measured on a real Dart corpus — quantifying it is the first step of the fix, not a claim being made here.\n\nHole recorded as a strict xfail; fixing the analyzer XPASSes the cell and forces the entry's removal in the same PR."  # 1643
    fields: {}  # 1643
